00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:33.980
Please explain how are
your companies benefiting

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.640
from the Cloud
Automation Platform.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:41.820
What are the key challenges that
you're facing at this stage,

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:46.580
and what are the benefits that
you are drawing from working

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.710
together in that ecosystem?

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:54.140
And maybe let's get
started with MIR,

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:57.890
getting from the bottom of this
[? talk, ?] from the hardware.

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:03.800
NIELS JACOBSEN: I can say
that at MIR, we develop

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.640
and manufacture what we
call Autonomous Mobile

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.400
Robots, or AMR.

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:14.570
And we do that for
indoor transportation

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:19.040
to help the logistic there,
to optimize logistic and also,

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:22.190
as we heard before, actually to
free some of the working force

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:23.720
there.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:26.870
So some of the
challenges we see there,

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:28.940
as you have already
mentioned actually,

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:31.760
is the cost of integration.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:35.270
We are distributing
all our robots

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:38.420
through a network
of integrators.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:41.240
And they are taking the
robot to the end users,

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:43.700
integrating that
for that purpose.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:48.830
And it still takes a lot
of time and is costly,

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:52.880
even though we have a simple
system to operate on there.

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:56.130
And then you can also say that
sometimes that the customer,

00:25:56.130 --> 00:26:01.160
there always are a new way
of using the robots that they

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:02.000
would like.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.835
And that is always
something that you not

00:26:06.835 --> 00:26:08.840
can provide out of the box.

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:10.490
Then the customer
has to wait-- either

00:26:10.490 --> 00:26:13.100
for a software
upgrade from our side

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:15.660
or find another
solution for that.

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:19.640
And then we have the last
[? modern ?] [? stength ?] is

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:23.750
the integration with ERP or
warehouse management system

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:24.630
there.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:27.650
We also see that there tends
to be a lot of specialized

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:29.420
software there.

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:32.270
And here we really see
that this cloud solution

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:35.750
can go in and help us on
many of the points there.

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:39.980
This ability to have
versioning and updating

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:43.100
of the system, more
or less for free.

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:47.840
We also see that with
a cloud platform,

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.570
the ability to have
data aggregated

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:54.150
to the cloud in a way
that you can do analysis

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.110
is now possible on
a much larger scale.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.530
And also the ability
to have add-in

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:02.810
[? plugs, ?] like
an SAP integration,

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.430
can be of great help for us.

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:08.390
EWA DUERR: You mentioned
SAP and the integration

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:10.230
with the ERP system.

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:11.810
What is SAP saying on that?

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:14.450
What are the key challenges
that you are facing?

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:17.930
And building on that point,
exactly how are you benefiting

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:21.890
from working with
us, and with Niels,

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.130
and all the other partners?

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:24.720
Rudy?

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:26.178
NEMRUDE VERZANO:
Yeah, the thing is

00:27:26.178 --> 00:27:29.170
that SAP has a lot of
customers-- not only

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:31.460
in a [INAUDIBLE]
management environment.

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:33.920
And more and more
of our customers

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:37.580
are interested in making
use of collaborative robots.

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:39.740
And the thing is, usually,
also our customers

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:42.500
have different kinds of
specific processes they

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:45.990
want to have be customized
depending on which areas

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:49.340
they are-- like mining,
manufacturing, discrete

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:52.620
and processing
industries, or logistics.

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:55.770
The thing, of course, is that,
looking at those devices--

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:58.740
those robots-- they
have different kinds

00:27:58.740 --> 00:27:59.730
of capabilities.

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.740
Compared to static robots,
they can be trained.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:06.860
They can repeat their
processes over and over again.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:11.670
Why you have seen it on the
pictures-- on the video--

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.170
that they have
more capabilities,

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:17.800
more dynamic features that
need to be considered.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:20.490
So in our case, we cannot
really cover everything.

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:22.950
And therefore, we need a
kind of flexible environment

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:26.650
where we can also integrate
to customers, partners,

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:29.050
and third-party
solutions easily.

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:30.930
And that's what the
Cloud Robotics Platform

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:35.220
will help us to cope with
all kinds of challenges

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:37.230
that are related to
collaborative robots

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:38.370
in this case.

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:40.800
Just as an example,
if [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:43.620
is going to make use
of further fleets

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:46.620
that are not from
MIR or from Magazino,

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:49.560
then the challenge will
be to orchestrate them.

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:50.130
OK?

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:51.990
And to orchestrate
them is one thing.

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:55.740
But to assign the tasks from
the back end accordingly

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:57.870
will be the next thing.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:00.780
But to do it over and over
again for every customer

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:02.560
would not make sense at all.

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:04.890
So we will be
definitely benefiting

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:06.660
from this Cloud
Robotics initiative

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:11.580
to plug-in existing solutions--
even custom-developed solutions

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:13.200
for specific customers.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.720
And we can deploy
them easily regardless

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.960
if that component is being
deployed on the robot,

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:23.030
on the cloud, on-prem depending
on the use case of course.

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:26.243
And we will definitely
be more flexible

00:29:26.243 --> 00:29:27.285
than we were in the past.

00:29:27.285 --> 00:29:31.080
We'll integrate all
kinds of devices.

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.070
That is our point.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:35.410
EWA DUERR: Building on
the point of both Niels--

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:38.590
and Rudy mentioned cloud--

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:42.010
Felix, you are gathering
with [? enabler ?]

00:29:42.010 --> 00:29:44.440
of the digital
twin-- a lot of data.

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:46.990
How are you benefiting
from the cloud

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:52.832
and from the Cloud Automation
Platform at this stage?

00:29:52.832 --> 00:29:54.290
FELIX REINSHAGEN:
First, let me say

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:56.600
a few words on what
we are doing at Navvis

00:29:56.600 --> 00:29:58.340
because this whole
term of digital twin

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:00.108
is gaining a lot of momentum.

00:30:00.108 --> 00:30:02.400
And I think it still needs
a little bit of explanation.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:06.350
So what we are doing is we
do not model environment,

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:07.700
but we scan environment.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.380
And we scan it on
mobile devices--

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:13.070
think about the mini
version of the famous Google

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:16.310
Car, now capable of
working without GPS

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:17.840
and equipped scanners,
with cameras.

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:23.540
And it creates super
dense and detailed 3D maps

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:26.900
of its environment that we then
overlay with more information

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:30.710
and make walkable digital
twins out of that that you

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:32.825
can access in your browser.

00:30:32.825 --> 00:30:33.700
And that's the point.

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:39.020
Today, we are creating already
digital twins of manufacturing

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:42.590
facilities for a lot of the
largest automotive companies

00:30:42.590 --> 00:30:44.240
in the world.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:46.970
But the data is being created
and processed in a form

00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:49.820
that it's most catering
towards human beings--

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:52.880
for space optimization,
logistics optimization.

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:54.380
However, of course,
we want to make

00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:58.520
this data accessible for
our clients in a much more

00:30:58.520 --> 00:30:59.552
flexible way.

00:30:59.552 --> 00:31:01.010
And we believe
that, in the future,

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:04.040
this data will perhaps
even be used by more

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.020
robots than human beings.

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:07.967
And then, all the
points come into play

00:31:07.967 --> 00:31:09.050
that you mentioned before.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:12.380
There will be very
different kinds of robots.

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:14.630
And they all need to be
capable of accessing,

00:31:14.630 --> 00:31:16.400
not only our data,
but potentially,

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:17.900
as well, other model data.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:21.050
So at the end, we
need good automation.

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:22.700
We need standardized
interfaces that

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:26.090
allow for a variety
of different robots,

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:29.820
as well as software solutions
to make use of this data.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.820
That will make our clients
happy and, of course,

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:33.880
will drive the adoption
of what we are doing.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:36.010
So that's why we're here.

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:40.000
EWA DUERR: Yeah, and
building on that, Moritz,

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:44.380
you are not only building
hardware, but also software.

00:31:44.380 --> 00:31:46.930
What are the key challenges
that you are facing?

00:31:46.930 --> 00:31:50.940
And how is Cloud
helping you do that?

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:52.690
MORITZ TENORTH: Yeah,
so with our company,

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:55.270
we're building the hardware,
but especially all the software

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:57.603
in the cloud integration for
mobile robots that not only

00:31:57.603 --> 00:31:59.760
navigate and transport,
but also pick.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:03.565
And these robots can
operate alongside the humans

00:32:03.565 --> 00:32:04.690
in these same environments.

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:07.540
So they can drive through the
warehouses, identify the items,

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:11.110
pick from the shelves,
stow into the shelves,

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:12.910
and, basically, do all
of this in a shared

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:14.285
environment with
humans that pick

00:32:14.285 --> 00:32:16.255
the same items from the
same shelves and so on.

00:32:16.255 --> 00:32:18.130
And especially this
collaboration with humans

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:19.750
creates lots of challenges.

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:22.030
Because humans change
the world, and compared

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:23.440
to a classical
robots that really

00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:26.230
like it when everything is
deterministic and predefined,

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:27.730
we live in a pretty
uncertain world.

00:32:27.730 --> 00:32:28.855
We're still in a warehouse.

00:32:28.855 --> 00:32:31.138
We're not in a children's
room in the afternoon.

00:32:31.138 --> 00:32:32.680
That is much more
uncertain probably,

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:35.990
but the world is
changing constantly.

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:39.580
And the big challenge is
how do you, on the one hand,

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:42.220
design software
that scalable, that

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:44.853
is capable of analyzing
this data, that can

00:32:44.853 --> 00:32:47.020
perceive the world into and
devise the right actions

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:47.720
from that?

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:49.570
How do you develop and
deploy this software

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:53.310
to a fleet of robots that are
somewhere in the warehouses?

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:55.060
But the big challenge,
I think, that we're

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:57.708
sharing with other people
dealing with autonomous robots

00:32:57.708 --> 00:32:59.500
is basically how to
make them more robust--

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:02.680
so how to make it resilient
to changes, to perturbations,

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:07.097
to all kinds of changes that
the robots have to cope with.

00:33:07.097 --> 00:33:08.680
And that's a pretty
data-driven thing.

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:11.372
So the developers don't
sit next to the robots.

00:33:11.372 --> 00:33:12.580
They don't see what happened.

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:15.560
So you really need to
transfer all this context--

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:17.980
all this information-- from
the robots to the developers

00:33:17.980 --> 00:33:20.363
or to your machine
learning algorithms.

00:33:20.363 --> 00:33:22.030
And that's something
where we can really

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:23.710
benefit from the cloud I think.

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:27.520
On the one hand, for using
computation in the cloud--

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:30.150
on the other hand also
for using data storage.

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:31.595
So how can we stream the data?

00:33:31.595 --> 00:33:33.220
I'm going to make it
available for easy

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:35.830
debugging-- also make it
available for automated machine

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.260
learning on that.

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:41.440
And another track is definitely
how can we use Cloud technology

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:44.380
in order to deploy
software on the robots

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:46.810
on the on-premise service
and the cloud service.

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:48.640
And for example, with
the Cloud Engine,

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:52.030
how do we deploy applications
on all of these different layers

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:52.870
at the same time?

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:54.340
How do we roll it
back and forth?

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:57.760
So that's super useful for us.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:00.970
EWA DUERR: OK, and that's
definitely the key point here

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:04.190
is the integration between
the hardware and the software.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:07.440
And coming back to the
hardware once more,

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:11.469
Niels, how do you see the future
for collaborative robotics?

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:15.370
And how can this open platform
and open ecosystem can help you

00:34:15.370 --> 00:34:19.217
grow the market in the future?

00:34:19.217 --> 00:34:20.800
NIELS JACOBSEN: You
can say, in a way,

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:24.460
we've already seen
with our mobile robot

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:26.530
that there's a
whole community now,

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:28.960
building what we
call top modules.

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:33.190
So that is different platforms
you put on top of robots.

00:34:33.190 --> 00:34:34.060
And--

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:36.880
EWA DUERR: We've seen
that in the video as well.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:39.010
NIELS JACOBSEN: But it's
still limited in a way,

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:42.290
because we cannot add new
software to it in an easy way.

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:44.739
So it's my hope that
we, in this way,

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:49.179
can envision what actually is
becoming a community that can

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:51.929
provide new solutions for us--

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:53.739
not only on the
hardware, but also

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:57.040
the capability of moving
the robot in another way.

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:01.150
We can have someone following
people knowing what to do.

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:05.560
And this can actually be a
business where these software

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.530
companies are [? leaving ?]
from just creating

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:14.460
this additional toolset for
different kinds of vendors.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:20.290
We see that as a hope for that,
because we have many ones today

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:23.257
that want to build more
really good solutions,

00:35:23.257 --> 00:35:25.090
but are just too small
of companies for that

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:28.780
because this environment
is just too spread.

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:33.070
Then you also see this ability
to move a lot of the computing

00:35:33.070 --> 00:35:34.615
from the robot into the cloud.

00:35:34.615 --> 00:35:35.240
EWA DUERR: Hmm.

00:35:35.240 --> 00:35:37.390
NIELS JACOBSEN: It's
something we have been

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:39.800
hoping for more than 10 years.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:41.590
But now we think that
the foundation now

00:35:41.590 --> 00:35:43.730
is more or less ready.

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:46.330
So we can see the
first thing there.

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:49.690
Things like a planning
how the robot is moving

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:52.120
from in the warehouse there.

00:35:52.120 --> 00:35:54.190
Of course, a robot on
their own can do that.

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:57.220
But now we have several hundreds
of robots moving together--

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:00.040
how this orchestration, as
we talked on before, how that

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:01.240
can be done.

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:04.798
That just makes a demand for
much more computing power.

00:36:04.798 --> 00:36:06.340
That shouldn't be
on the robot there,

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:11.560
but should be on some kind
of cloud computing platform.

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:15.070
And last, [INAUDIBLE] we just
released our first AI product

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:16.150
this week.

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:19.730
So AI is really coming
onto the robots there,

00:36:19.730 --> 00:36:21.550
but we'll see much more of that.

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:24.440
This is really a field that's
moving very, very fast.

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:26.920
And I don't think any
vendor can sit down and say,

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:28.270
we can do it the best.

00:36:28.270 --> 00:36:31.295
We need to be open and see
what others can come up with.

00:36:31.295 --> 00:36:33.670
And we really feel-- and also
some of the things you have

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:35.200
presented this week here--

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:37.780
that these are really,
really good platforms

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:40.657
on how the robots
can learn to interact

00:36:40.657 --> 00:36:41.740
much more with the people.

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:46.660
So we really hope that the
cloud robotics platform will

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:48.710
be the right tool for that.

00:36:48.710 --> 00:36:51.400
EWA DUERR: So enabling
the deployment

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:56.650
and the distribution of new
services-- new applications.

00:36:56.650 --> 00:37:00.520
Moritz, you mentioned you are
building a lot of software

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:04.660
for your robots and
for your hardware.

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:07.810
How will that help you in
deployment and distribution?

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:10.230
It that the Kubernetes that is--

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:11.730
MORITZ TENORTH:
Yeah, I think it's--

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:12.900
EWA DUERR: --technology here?

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:15.192
MORITZ TENORTH: So Kubernetes,
at the different layers,

00:37:15.192 --> 00:37:18.220
really promises to
facilitate this--

00:37:18.220 --> 00:37:20.680
the whole DevOps
tasks that you have

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:23.650
to do on many robots
that are in the fields

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:26.207
with different internet
connectivity levels,

00:37:26.207 --> 00:37:28.040
also having to some
on-premise installations

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:29.990
some cloud
installations, and so on.

00:37:29.990 --> 00:37:32.230
And that can be
[? pretty ?] challenging

00:37:32.230 --> 00:37:34.610
if you have large fleets--
also different hardware.

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:36.055
So it's not all virtualized.

00:37:36.055 --> 00:37:39.250
You really have physical
devices behind that that are

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:40.880
mechanically different there.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:43.930
And really deploying to
these things, managing them,

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:46.000
having the fleets
and data management--

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:50.140
I think that is one big thing
that we can benefit from.

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:51.820
Another one is to
really use the cloud

00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:54.830
capabilities for making these
robots more intelligent, more

00:37:54.830 --> 00:37:55.330
robust.

00:37:55.330 --> 00:37:58.030
So doing more machine
learning in the cloud,

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:00.640
but also using existing
services from the cloud

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:03.290
and integrating
them more easily.

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:05.290
And I think third part
is what's also planned

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:07.082
for the robotic platform
to have the market

00:38:07.082 --> 00:38:10.900
place so that we can
benefit from other people's

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:12.130
components--

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:15.370
for example, the localization
component, also some image

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:17.445
recognition, or parts.

00:38:17.445 --> 00:38:20.170
But we can also use it for
sharing our own applications

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:20.930
with others.

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:24.418
So if other robot vendors
can use certain controller

00:38:24.418 --> 00:38:25.960
components, for
example, that we have

00:38:25.960 --> 00:38:29.380
a way of sharing that and
also sharing the experiences

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:33.040
we made there on solving
these real-world problems.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:36.760
EWA DUERR: And building on
the application marketplace,

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:39.520
what are the benefits
in the future for you,

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:44.010
Felix, and for Navvis, as you
engage on that marketplace

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:46.097
and in that ecosystem?

00:38:46.097 --> 00:38:47.930
FELIX REINSHAGEN: So
just give an impression

00:38:47.930 --> 00:38:51.930
on what we create-- it's
layers and layers of data.

00:38:51.930 --> 00:38:54.260
It starts as really
the replication

00:38:54.260 --> 00:38:56.930
of the physical reality and
point clouds and images that

00:38:56.930 --> 00:39:00.260
then transform into 3D models.

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:02.570
And each step, it's
kind of a data refinery.

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:04.070
It puts layers and
layers of data.

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:06.830
Like you started
with raw oil and end

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:08.945
up with all the different
plastics products.

00:39:08.945 --> 00:39:10.820
And of course, on all
these different stages,

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:12.650
you have a very complex
processing-- a lot

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:14.610
of machine learning and AI.

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:16.410
A lot of that, we do ourselves.

00:39:16.410 --> 00:39:18.570
But you are spreading
yourself very thin

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:20.860
if you're trying to do
all of that yourself.

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:23.840
And as well, all these
intermediate products

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:28.230
are very important
raw material, so

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:31.770
to say, for a lot of
other applications.

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:33.620
And on all these
different layers,

00:39:33.620 --> 00:39:35.630
you encounter the problem
of standardizations

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:36.860
and interfacing.

00:39:36.860 --> 00:39:39.500
And of course, we are talking
about vast amounts of data.

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:42.690
We are, by now, scanning
tens of millions

00:39:42.690 --> 00:39:46.770
of square feet of data
every single month.

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:49.230
Some of our clients,
with hundreds

00:39:49.230 --> 00:39:51.450
of millions of square
feet of industrial space,

00:39:51.450 --> 00:39:54.750
now moved from scanning,
perhaps once every six months,

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:56.140
to every month.

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:58.540
And in the future, they
want to scan every day.

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:01.650
So they want to have something
like a real-time digital twin.

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:04.530
Only a very scalable and
robust cloud platform

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:06.690
can actually handle
this kind of data--

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:09.750
run continuously
through all these stages

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:12.010
of data processing.

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:14.130
So it's a matter of
scalability, absolutely.

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:16.260
It's a matter of
interoperability

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.420
with all the different players.

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:21.630
And of course, I think at the
end, you need a marketplace.

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:24.690
Of course, it will be myriads
of different applications

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:28.980
that do this data refinement
and, as well, take that data

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:30.750
and move it on
each of the layers

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:32.430
between different applications.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:35.520
So yeah, I think that
is why we believe

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:41.160
that cloud-- or a large-scale
automated robotic platforms--

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:42.970
will play such an
important role for us.

00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:45.540
And I think it's not even
possible to move that kind

00:40:45.540 --> 00:40:48.380
of ecosystem forward without.

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:52.980
EWA DUERR: And coming
back to SAP-- and to Rudy,

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:55.620
maybe-- and building on
that point of creating

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:59.310
the marketplace and leveraging
different applications

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:03.750
for the business processes,
how are ERP systems

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:07.770
and SAP dealing with that
and benefiting from that?

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:13.620
And also, SAP and Google have a
broader strategic partnership,

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:17.880
so how does this project fit
into the overall relationship

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:21.180
and partnership
between Google and SAP?

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:23.167
Maybe you can comment on that.

00:41:23.167 --> 00:41:25.500
NEMRUDE VERZANO: I think this
project has already proven

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:28.340
that the combined setup--

00:41:28.340 --> 00:41:33.690
having Google as a kind
of infrastructure provider

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:35.910
to us in this robotics project--

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:38.490
and us being able to
concentrate on the business

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:42.960
processes only
allowed us then indeed

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:49.230
to ignore all this
communication, data storage,

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:52.500
and data management-- or
device management stuff--

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.860
that all of you, of course,
need to take care of.

00:41:55.860 --> 00:42:00.150
But it allowed us
to concentrate--

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:01.350
to keep the core focus--

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:04.290
on the improvements of things
that we want to extend.

00:42:04.290 --> 00:42:07.230
In our case, to get the
warehouse management

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:10.640
being connected to the
robots very easily.

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:13.620
And that we can
use infrastructures

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:19.230
that are easy to integrate--

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:21.660
also to deploy the different
components and containers

00:42:21.660 --> 00:42:22.890
very easily.

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:26.940
And that we can identify easily
which components are used

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:29.160
for the specific use cases.

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:31.500
This allows us, of
course, on our journey

00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:35.460
to support our
customers regarding

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:39.070
the intelligent enterprises
that most of our customers

00:42:39.070 --> 00:42:40.530
are up to.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:43.560
Also to include
further capabilities

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:48.700
that the Google Cloud
Platform is providing.

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:51.330
As you could see, on
the videos, for example,

00:42:51.330 --> 00:42:55.950
the machine learning-based
vision service that Google is

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:57.360
providing--

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:00.420
we can integrate
easily into scenarios

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:06.180
and use cases without having two
weeks or two months of customer

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:07.500
development.

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:10.650
That will definitely
be of very big benefit

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:15.330
to many of our customers if they
want it to include and enhance

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:16.830
their processes.

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:20.550
And usually any
customer that we have

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:24.210
is somewhat interested in
integrating more and more

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:27.030
of the AI and ML capabilities.

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:29.640
So at the moment, we can
start with the foundation

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:33.120
that we have already in place.

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:36.070
But depending on the
use case the customer

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:39.110
is interested in, we can
pick different components

00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:41.010
that are, for
example, on the Cloud

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:45.120
Platform, or our customer, or
even our partner is providing

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:49.560
and integrate it into
the process very easily.

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:56.490
And compared to former times, it
is by far easier now to do so.

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:59.280
And especially also in
conjunction with the Industry

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:03.510
4.0 initiative in Germany
that I'm also driving,

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:06.870
our goal is to have an
environment where we can do

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:09.210
plug-and-play of devices--

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:12.960
of robots-- that our
customers can easily

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:16.870
integrate the devices in their
processes in their business,

00:44:16.870 --> 00:44:20.760
but also make use of
intelligence services

00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:22.933
that can be coming, of
course, from Google,

00:44:22.933 --> 00:44:24.600
from the Google Cloud
Platform, but also

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:26.910
from embedded
business applications

00:44:26.910 --> 00:44:28.410
that we already have in place.

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:32.130
So therefore, it is definitely
a complementary setup

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:35.760
to enable our customers
accomplishing the goal

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:39.450
to have an intelligent
enterprise in the near future.

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:41.490
EWA DUERR: OK, and
definitely Google

00:44:41.490 --> 00:44:43.460
is, as well, on
the positive note,

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:46.800
benefiting from the
partnership with SAP

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:51.370
with the business knowledge
in the B2B applications.

00:44:51.370 --> 00:44:54.790
So I think we all can
benefit from that.

00:44:54.790 --> 00:44:58.810
And since we are
running out of time,

00:44:58.810 --> 00:45:00.480
I don't think there
is a better way

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:03.330
to end that panel
than just by saying

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:08.160
that we, as Google, are only as
successful as our partners are.

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:11.730
So we are happy that you
are happy, so to speak.

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:15.900
And for our clients, like
Bechtle, we definitely

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:21.810
want to drive business impact,
drive efficiency, scalability,

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:24.870
flexibility for our
partners, whether they

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:29.370
are startups, developers, ERP
providers, and so on and so

00:45:29.370 --> 00:45:30.000
forth.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.450
We definitely want to enable
new business opportunities

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:38.010
and enable you to reap
new revenue sources

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:40.440
and grow the market reach.

00:45:40.440 --> 00:45:45.950
So that's our common goal,
I think, for all of us.

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:47.670
So on that positive
note, thank you

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:49.540
very much for your attention.

00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:52.390
And feel free to reach out
to us after the session

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:53.770
if you have any questions.

00:45:53.770 --> 00:45:54.780
Thank you.

